-- Pour-rate starting point (~5 g/s), not a reproduction of this source's recipe:
-- https://coffeeadastra.com/2020/05/23/the-physics-of-kettle-streams/

= Prep =
Place the three-layer side of the filter against the spout. Rinse, then discard the rinse water. Add @coffee{30%g} to the #Chemex{} and make a small well in the center. Larger batches may need a slightly coarser grind. Watch the filter and carafe levels; pause before overflowing and split the batch if needed near the upper end of the dose range.

= Bloom =
Wet all the grounds with @water{90%g} over about ~scaled{18%seconds}, then give the brewer a gentle swirl.

= Let it bloom =
Let the coffee release its gas. No water needed. Wait until ~until{45%seconds} on the brew clock.

= Pour 1 =
Pour @water{130%g} over about ~scaled{26%seconds} in steady circles, from the center outward. Aim for a comfortable stream, not an exact rate. Avoid the filter walls.

= Let it settle =
Give the water time to drain through the bed for ~{20%seconds}.

= Pour 2 =
Pour @water{130%g} over about ~scaled{26%seconds} in steady circles, from the center outward. Aim for a comfortable stream, not an exact rate. Avoid the filter walls.

= Let it settle =
Give the water time to drain through the bed for ~{20%seconds}.

= Pour 3 =
Pour @water{130%g} over about ~scaled{26%seconds} in steady circles, from the center outward. Aim for a comfortable stream, not an exact rate. Avoid the filter walls.

= Draw down =
Gently swirl to level the bed. Allow about ~{107%seconds} for the remaining water to drain. Larger batches may need longer; finish when drained, not at a fixed clock time.

= Finish =
Swirl, sip, and tell your journal how it went.
